{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Let every man ask of his fellow.</b> This asking is not like the borrowing mentioned in connection with utensils, which is a loan on condition to return; rather, He commanded that they ask of them as a gift, and the Holy One, blessed be He, would give them favor in the eyes of the Egyptians and they would give to them. And this is not theft of mind (deception) that the Holy One, blessed be He, would command, heaven forbid; rather, the matter was permitted to them, for the labor that the Egyptians extracted from them has no value, and the wage of that labor has no end or limit. And is this not the law of the Torah regarding a slave who served his master for seven years, that he is obligated in the grant (ha'anakah), as it is said (Deuteronomy 15:13–14): ״וכי תשלחנו חפשי מעמך לא תשלחנו ריקם העניק תעניק לו מצאנך ומגרנך ומיקבך״ — all the more so the Egyptians, among whom the labor of Israel lasted של רד״ו years. And as for the fact that the expression רעהו (his fellow) and רעותה (her fellow) is used, it appears to me that before the giving of the Torah all creatures were companions together as one; but after the giving of the Torah, when the Holy One, blessed be He, offered the Torah to every nation and tongue and they did not accept it until Israel accepted it, all the nations departed from the brotherhood and fellowship, and this name remained with the people of Israel alone, who are called brothers and fellows to the Place — as it is written (Psalms 122:8): ״למען אחי ורעי״ — and this is what the Rabbis of blessed memory expounded (Deuteronomy 22:3): ״לכל אבדת אחיך״ — 'your brother' and not the gentile; and likewise (Deuteronomy 23:20): ״לא תשיך לאחיך״ — 'your brother' and not the gentile."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:57:47.180463285Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}